{"qid":"Q83546","names":["Principality of Rostov-Suzdal","Principality of Vladimir-Suzdal"],"title":"Principality of Rostov-Suzdal","endonym":{"native":"Великое княжество Владимирское","lang":"ru"},"capsule":"The Principality of Vladimir-Suzdal (earlier Rostov-Suzdal) was the dominant Rus' principality of northeastern Russia from the mid-11th century, centered first at Rostov and Suzdal and then, from 1157, at Vladimir on the Klyazma River when Andrei Bogolyubsky moved the capital there. Under Andrei (r. 1157–1174) and his brother Vsevolod III 'the Big Nest' (r. 1176–1212), the principality asserted supremacy over other Rus' princes, sacked Kyiv in 1169, and developed a distinctive architectural tradition exemplified by the Cathedral of the Assumption and the Church of the Intercession on the Nerl. The Mongol invasion of 1237–1238 under Batu Khan devastated Vladimir, killed Grand Prince Yuri II at the Battle of the Sit River in 1238, and reduced the principality to vassal status under the Golden Horde.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The principality's core territorial extent is reasonably well documented in chronicle sources; the outer boundaries of its northeastern frontier zone are approximately known.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Vladimir-Suzdal","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Vladimir-Suzdal"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Vladimir-Suzdal","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vladimir-Suzdal"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Vladimir-Suzdal","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Vladimir I of Kiev","nativeName":"Владимир Святославич","roman":"Vladimir Svyatoslavich","star